# Pi sandbox and permissions

Env-specific facts and decisions only, each at its current state with a dated
annotation. Generic harness behavior lives in the tool descriptions and the
workflow's own system-prompt injection — it does not belong here; sweep
measurements and audit baselines live in the chezmoi repo's
`docs/agents-audit-log.md`, not here. If behavior seems changed, verify
against the source repository and update this file.

## Planning and permissions (last verified 2026-09-12)

- New sessions start in plan mode with auto-reviewed approvals. Source writes
  remain disabled; temporary build/cache artifacts use designated writable roots.
  The mode asks for the work as well as forbidding the edits: research the
  request to the point of a plan unprompted, delegate the independent
  exploration, ask where different readings would lead to materially different
  work, then submit the plan without being asked for it.
- `/execute` or approval of `submit_plan` explicitly enables scoped execution.
  Every mode switch revokes child sessions and sandbox processes before the
  new mode takes effect — `/plan` before enabling planning, and approval of
  `submit_plan` the same way before enabling execution, so a child still
  running when the plan is approved ends aborted.
- `/approvals` selects auto review or individual prompts. Hard denial, inherited
  role scope and mode restrictions run before approval; approval cannot widen them.
- Shell commands run inside the SRT profile without review, as sandboxed
  commands do in Claude Code and Codex; the profile is the boundary. Only
  remote-mutating verbs the profile cannot judge — `git push`, `gh` other than
  view/list/status/search/diff/checks, `gh api` with a method or field,
  `npm publish`, `docker push`, `curl`/`wget` with data, json or upload flags,
  `ssh`, `scp`, `sftp`, `rsync` — go to the auto classifier, then to a confirm
  dialog on `ask`. File edits inside the workspace run without review once
  execution is approved.
- `workspace_bash` with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` (Claude Code's flag)
  runs the command on the host outside the SRT profile, under the host
  environment minus the workflow's own variables. Every such call is reviewed,
  plan mode included, and never takes the sandboxed auto-allow: the classifier
  under auto approvals, the confirm dialog (`Approve this unsandboxed action
  once?`) under ask or on a classifier `ask`. Read-only roles are refused. The
  row reads `Ran … · unsandboxed`. The model sets it only after a
  sandbox-attributable failure of that command or on request, per command;
  `!` commands stay sandboxed. The docker socket is one such failure: no
  `--config` or `DOCKER_HOST` workaround reaches it from inside the profile
  (verified 2026-09-10).
- Temp files inside the profile: `$TMPDIR` is the lease's scratch directory
  (srt would otherwise export `/tmp/claude`, which the profile never allows),
  so `mktemp -d "$TMPDIR/x.XXXXXX"` works while a bare `mktemp -d` does not —
  macOS `mktemp` ignores `TMPDIR` without a template and writes to the
  per-user `/var/folders` dir, which stays denied. Apple's `/bin/bash` 3.2
  puts here-document temp files in `/var/tmp` (writable for every role on
  macOS for exactly this) rather than `$TMPDIR`. Verified 2026-09-11. The classifier also sees the session's recent
  shell commands (the command, whether it ran sandboxed, its exit code; never
  its output), so a retry after a sandboxed failure is judged on that evidence.
- Classifier errors/uncertainty prompt the root session; unattended requests deny.
  A pending dialog is announced in the footer and times out after ten minutes.
- `ask_user_question` (the pinned `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` plugin)
  presents up to four questions with options, previews and free text in one
  tabbed dialog; answers land in the transcript and the classifier's task
  context. Approval dialogs are once-only; MCP approvals are never cached
  across a mode change.
- `workspace_bash` with `run_in_background: true` starts a background task
  (Claude Code's flag). The call is held open for 10 s first: a command that
  ends inside that grace is answered like a foreground call and never
  notifies — most backgrounded commands end there, and each completion past
  the grace wakes an idle parent for a full turn (verified 2026-09-10, the
  cost that motivated the grace). A longer one returns its task id,
  the worker keeps its lease, and when the command ends the model hears a
  steer message with the exit status and the last twenty lines (with its
  next tool result, or as a new turn when idle). `workspace_task`
  `{ id, action: "output" | "stop" }` reads the output so far or stops it. Output is kept in memory, tail-capped
  at 256 KiB. `/plan` and `/remove-dir` stop running tasks like any sandbox
  process (they end as `stopped`); so does exiting pi.
- `/add-dir <path>` adds a directory to the editable workspace, as Claude
  Code's does: edits inside it are allowed once execution is approved, its
  `.git`, `.pi` and every relative deny entry (`.env`) are protected there as
  under cwd, and its `AGENTS.md` (or
  `CLAUDE.md`) is appended to the system prompt after passing the read policy
  (a symlink out of the directory or onto a denied path refuses the add).
  Home, its ancestors, denied paths and directories already in scope are
  refused with the reason. `/remove-dir <path>` drops one. Both take their
  argument inline; Tab lists what each accepts (the addable directories, the
  added ones) and, once accepted, lists the next level down, so Tab walks the
  tree. State is per session; skills
  under an added directory need a restart with `--skill <path>`, since pi
  discovers resources only at startup and `/reload`. Reads were never
  confined to the workspace; only the deny list applies.
- If a process disappears without acknowledging termination, the workflow blocks.
  Check orphan processes and restart; do not treat a lost connection as proof that
  a writer stopped.

The `workspace_*` tools are the SDK's own tools running in the host Pi process;
each invocation leases a per-tool SRT worker that performs the actual file and
shell operations, so effects and subprocess execution are sandboxed — not all
file I/O (path probes, diff generation, and output temp files stay host-side).
Every lease consumes a single-use ticket minted by its approval, so a mode
change between approval and execution invalidates the lease. Interactive `!`
commands use the same worker boundary. MCP stdio servers also run inside SRT,
while OAuth stays in the trusted host Pi process.

Sensitive paths come from the shared denylist. File tools additionally resolve
symlinks and confine edits to the workspace; git metadata and live-loaded code
(`~/.pi/agent`, the repository `node_modules`, `~/.zshrc`) are write-protected
in every mode, while the repository source is editable once execution is
approved. A refusal names its reason (`Writes are disabled in this scope`,
`Path denied by managed policy`, `File edits must remain inside the
workspace`); the model should report it rather than re-submit the plan. This is
an explicit denylist, not a guarantee against unknown credential locations. Add
new credential paths to shared policy before exposing them to a session.

Extensions are trusted host code. Per-tool SRT is not containment against a
malicious extension. The reviewed `dangerouslyDisableSandbox` flag above is the
only unsandboxed path in the managed tools. Linux needs bubblewrap, socat and
ripgrep; macOS needs sandbox-exec.
